So fix the interpreter.  That sort of behavior is completely broken.
Spaces are valid in filenames, whether you like it or not.

> Not 5 minutes ago, a friend asked me to download something from his ftp
> site.  It had a space in the file name.  I had him change the name -- it
> was easier than figuring out where I had to put quotes.

Put quotes around the entire filename.  It's not difficult.

> Spaces are delimiters.  They simply do not belong in any file name.  Use
> underscores, use more capital letters, but don't use spaces.  It's just
> asking for trouble.

Just about anything can be a delimiter in *some* application.  Should
commas be acceptable?  Semicolons?  Colons?  Forward Slashes on
windows?  Backslashes on Unix? 

Hell, periods are namespace delimiters in many programming languages,
and someone might want to map filesystem namespaces onto a
programming language's.  Let's not use periods, either!

File and directory names are arbitrary strings, apart from the
filesystem's own namespace delimiter (and I'd rather do without even
that).  Any software that does not take this into consideration, and
use some *other* way of delimiting things (or at the very least a
mechanism of quoting and/or escaping), is broken.

